.TH getty "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
getty \- read a login name and start login
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/getty [\fItype\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
getty -- read a login name and start login
Usage:	/etc/getty [ type ]
'type' selects the line-speed table to use on the terminal that is
the standard input; the default is table '0'.  A hard-wired speed
is selected with an upper-case letter 'A' through 'S'; other single
characters select tables of speeds appropriate to a dial-in modem,
which getty cycles through on each BREAK, blank input line, or
unrecognized character.
.HE
.B init
invokes
.B getty
on each enabled line listed in
.BR /etc/ttys ,
with the standard input, output, and error already attached to that
terminal, and with the second character of the corresponding
.B /etc/ttys
line (the character following the enable flag) as
.IR type .
.B getty
sets the terminal's initial speed and mode from the table selected
by
.IR type ,
prints a login prompt, and reads a name.
Once it has a usable name, it replaces itself with
.BR /bin/login ,
passing the name as its argument, and passing along the erase and
kill characters read from the terminal's previous settings.
.PP
While reading the name,
.B getty
folds the whole line to lower case if every letter typed was
upper case, recognizes the terminal's own erase and kill characters,
and, on BREAK, an empty line, or an unrecognized character, moves on
to the next speed in the table (wrapping back to the first) and
tries again.
An end-of-file character on the first character of the line, or a
NUL byte, also restarts the read at the next speed.
.PP
Without an argument,
.B getty
uses table
.BR 0 ,
a set of common dial-in speeds.
The recognized values of
.I type
select a table as follows:
.DS
.ta 8n
\fB-\fR	Teletype 33, fixed at 110 baud
\fB0\fR	300, 1200, 150, 110 baud, in that cycle
\fB1\fR	Teletype 37, fixed at 150 baud
\fB2\fR	9600 baud, fixed
\fB3\fR	1200, 300 baud, in that cycle
\fB4\fR	300 baud, fixed
\fB5\fR	300, 1200 baud, in that cycle
\fBA\fR	50 baud
\fBB\fR	75 baud
\fBC\fR	110 baud
\fBD\fR	134 baud
\fBE\fR	150 baud
\fBF\fR	200 baud
\fBG\fR	300 baud
\fBH\fR	600 baud
\fBI\fR	1200 baud
\fBJ\fR	1800 baud
\fBK\fR	2000 baud
\fBL\fR	2400 baud
\fBM\fR	3600 baud
\fBN\fR	4800 baud
\fBO\fR	7200 baud
\fBP\fR	9600 baud
\fBQ\fR	19200 baud
\fBR\fR, \fBS\fR	extended speeds A and B
.DE
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 12n
\fB/etc/ttys\fR	terminals to start getty on
\fB/bin/login\fR	program run once a name is read
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR init ,
.B login
.SH NOTES
.B getty
is always started by
.B init
with its argument taken from
.BR /etc/ttys ;
it is not ordinarily run by hand.
